`matches!` is a great tool to do the job of `.is_variant()` when not available, but it is not useful to replace `.as_variant()`, returning an `Option<T>`. It could be made possible, for instance by adding a rule with one more argument representing the value returned in the option. This new rule would be quite the same as the existing one.

```rust
macro_rules! matches {
    ($expression:expr, $(|)? $( $pattern:pat_param )|+ $( if $guard: expr )? $(,)?) => {
        match $expression {
            $( $pattern )|+ $( if $guard )? => true,
            _ => false
        }
    };
    ($expression:expr, $(|)? $( $pattern:pat_param )|+ $( if $guard: expr )?, $value:expr $(,)?) => {
        match $expression {
            $( $pattern )|+ $( if $guard )? => Some($value),
            _ => None,
        }
    };
}
```

It could enable to do:

```rust
let foo = matches!(my_enum, MyEnum::MyVariant(v, _), v)?;
```

instead of

```rust
let foo = match my_enum {
    MyEnum::MyVariant(v, _) => v,
    _ => return None,
}
```

It is maybe related to #86112 because the new rule of the macro would have to accept variable declarations in the pattern.

### Discussion

Maybe being able to return two different types according to the number of given arguments can be confusing, so maybe another macro could be defined instead.

Also, this macro rule is kind of a `map` because `$value` can by any expression, so maybe a new macro called `map!` could be created instead of adding a rule to `matches!`.

I suggest to add to the Rust stdlib implementations of gcd/binary_gcd/lcm. The ones belows are designed to be division-panic-free.

```rust
type T = u32;


// Postcondition: result == 0 => u == 0 || v == 0.
fn gcd(mut u: T, mut v: T) -> T {
    if v == 0 { return u; }
    loop {
        u %= v;
        if u == 0 { return v; }
        v %= u;
        if v == 0 { return u; }
    }
}


fn binary_gcd(mut u: T, mut v: T) -> T {
    if u == 0 { return v; }
    if v == 0 { return u; }
    let shift = (u | v).trailing_zeros();
    u >>= u.trailing_zeros();
    loop {
        v >>= v.trailing_zeros();
        if u > v {
            (u, v) = (v, u);
        }
        v -= u;
        if v == 0 { break; }
    }
    u << shift
}


fn lcm(a: T, b: T) -> T {
    let mut u = a;
    let mut v = b;
    if v == 0 { return u; }
    loop {
        u %= v;
        if u == 0 {
            return (a / v) * b;
        }
        v %= u;
        if v == 0 {
            return (a / u) * b;
        }
    }
}


//------------------------

fn main() {
	assert_eq!(gcd(10, 20), 10);
	assert_eq!(lcm(10, 20), 20);
	assert_eq!(binary_gcd(10, 20), 10);
}
```

gcd and lcm are short and used widely. Different implementations are possible, but their purpose and API won't change. They are present in the Python stdlib:
https://docs.python.org/3/library/math.html

The binary_gcd is faster in some cases, so I think it's useful to have both a simple gcd and a binary_gcd.

